<section xml:id="sec_what-is-magnetism" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>What Is Magnetism?</title>

		<activity>
      <title>Warm-up</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Write down something you know about magnets or magnetism, and how you know it. Where do you use magnets in your everyday life?</p>
		</activity>

		
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-magnetic-properties">
	<title>Magnetic Properties</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="K6zUQH4HGZ8"><description>A few experiments demonstrating basic magnetic properties.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>
	
	
	<p>Magnets interact in many interesting ways with different types of materials.  Key materials that exhibit prominent magnetic effects are permanent magnets and ferromagnetic materials.</p>
	
	<definition>
		<title>Permanent magnets</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p><term>Permanent magnets</term> are objects that have both attractive and repulsive interactions with each other. These objects have a <q>north</q> and a <q>south</q> pole that react to each other in different ways. Like poles repel and opposite poles attract.</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<definition>
		<title>Ferromagnetic materials</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p><term>Ferromagnetic</term> materials (like iron, cobalt, and nickel) tend to be attracted to permanent magnets.  Ferromagmetic materials can become permanent magnets through a process known as magnetization.</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>
	

		<corollary xml:id="vid-magnetism">
	<title>A Model for Magnetism</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="-1w7niP7m6E"><description>Introducing a model for magnetism.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

    <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>

  <definition>
	<title>
	Magnetic Field
	</title>
		<statement>
		
			<p>
			
			</p>
			
			<p>
			The <term> magnetic field </term> is a <em> vector field </em> that governs magnetic interactions.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The magnetic field is a vector.</alert>  Like other vectors, it has a magnitude and a direction.  Magnetic fields are measured in units of tesla (T).
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The magnetic field has a value at every point in space.</alert>  Either the magnitude or the direction (or both) can be different at different points in space.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Magnetic fields can be observed with a compass.</alert>  The north end of a compass needle will point in the direction of the magnetic field.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</statement>
</definition>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<p>The experiments below will help answer some of the model questions posed in the video above.</p>
		<activity>
      <title>Cutting a Magnet in Half</title>
      
		<statement>
			<p></p><p>Make a prediction about what you think happens if you cut a magnet in half.  Then watch the following video in the answer to check your prediction.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
	<url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q0d_SS-0Puk">What happens when you cut a magnet in half?</url>
			</answer>
		</activity>
		<activity>
      <title>Magnetic Grapes</title>
      
